# WordPress.com Studio

Every time you choose to apply a rule(s), explicitly state the rule(s) in the output. You can abbreviate the rule description to a single word or phrase.

## Project Context
A desktop Electron application for creating local WordPress environments, powered by WordPress Playground and WordPress.com. Used by WordPress enthusiasts and developers.

## Code Style and Structure
- Follow the WordPress coding standards
- Write concise, technical TypeScript code with accurate examples
- Prefer iteration and modularization over code duplication
- Use descriptive variable names with auxiliary verbs (e.g., isLoading, hasError)
- Structure repository files as follows:

```
src
├── about-menu         # Files required for the main about menu
├── components         # Shared React components
│   ├── icons          # Icons used in the application
│   └── tests          # Tests for shared components
├── hooks              # Custom React hooks
│   ├── sync-sites     # Hooks related to syncing local sites to WordPress.com
│   └── tests          # Tests for custom React hooks
├── lib                # Shared libraries
│   ├── import-export  # Shared libraries for import/export
│   └── tests          # Tests for shared libraries
├── migrations         # Migrations for application version upgrades
├── storage            # Utilities for storing persisted user data
│   └── tests          # Tests for storage utilities
├── tests              # Global test files
├── translations       # Translation JSON files
├── preload.ts         # The only Electron preload script (see https://www.electronjs.org/docs/latest/tutorial/process-model#preload-scripts)
└── ipc-handlers.ts    # Notable top-level file for handling Electron IPC messages
```

## Tech Stack
- React
- TypeScript
- Tailwind CSS
- Electron
- PHP

## Naming Conventions
- Use lowercase with dashes for directories (e.g., components/form-wizard)
- Favor named exports for components and utilities
- Use PascalCase for component files (e.g., ChatInput.tsx)
- Use camelCase for utility files (e.g., formValidator.ts)

## TypeScript Usage
- Use TypeScript for all code; prefer types over interfaces
- Use functional components with TypeScript interfaces
- Avoid try/catch blocks unless there's good reason to translate or handle error in that abstraction
- Use explicit return types for all functions
